<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eritrean War of Independence</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1961">1961</a>–<a href="/years/1991">1991</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Eritrean War of Independence was a thirty-year struggle by Eritrean nationalist fronts against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>, which had annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a> in 1962. Begun by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrean-liberation-front">Eritrean Liberation Front</a> in 1961, it was carried to victory by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrean-peoples-liberation-front">Eritrean People's Liberation Front</a>, which took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asmara">Asmara</a> in May 1991 as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derg">Derg</a> regime collapsed. Eritrea became formally independent after a referendum in 1993.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eritrean-war-of-independence.jpg" alt="Eritrean War of Independence" width="320" height="263" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Skilla1st · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eritrean_Independence_War_Map.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a> was an Italian colony from 1882 until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> conquered it in 1941, and then a British Military Administration until 1951. In 1952 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> decided to federate Eritrea with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>, which was to keep its own parliament under the Ethiopian crown. Eritrea's autonomy was curtailed during the 1950s as Unionist parties gained influence in the Eritrean Assembly. Exiles led by Idris Muhammad Adam founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrean-liberation-front">Eritrean Liberation Front</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> in 1961, and on 1 September 1961 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamid-idris-awate">Hamid Idris Awate</a> and his companions fought Ethiopian troops and police at Adal. In 1962 Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haile-selassie">Haile Selassie</a> dissolved the federation and the Eritrean parliament and annexed the country.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In the 1960s the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrean-liberation-front">Eritrean Liberation Front</a>, mostly Muslim and based in the lowlands, drew arms from Arab states, while many highland Christians distrusted it. The Ethiopian army answered by burning villages and shooting suspects. In 1970 splinter groups left the front, among them Christian fighters led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaias-afwerki">Isaias Afwerki</a>, and in August 1973 two of them formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrean-peoples-liberation-front">Eritrean People's Liberation Front</a>. The two organisations fought a civil war until popular pressure made them cooperate in 1975. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haile-selassie">Haile Selassie</a> was ousted in 1974, the Marxist Derg sent a peasant army of 100,000 against the rebels in 1975, and it was largely routed.</p><p>Between 1975 and 1977 the fronts held all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a> except <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asmara">Asmara</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massawa">Massawa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assab-bay">Assab</a> and Barentu. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ogaden-war">Ogaden War</a> the Ethiopian army, backed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, regained much of this ground, and in 1978 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrean-peoples-liberation-front">Eritrean People's Liberation Front</a> withdrew to the mountains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahel">Sahel</a> and held Nakfa. The Red Star Campaign of 1982, with more than 80,000 troops, failed to take it. In 1988 the front captured Afabet, and in February 1990 it took Massawa. Once the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> ended its support for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mengistu-haile-mariam">Mengistu Haile Mariam</a>, Ethiopian morale fell, and the garrison in Asmara disintegrated, letting the front enter the city on 24 May 1991.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Israel trained Ethiopian counter-insurgency forces, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> assisted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haile-selassie">Haile Selassie</a> until the revolution, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> aided the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derg">Derg</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ogaden-war">Ogaden War</a>. Iraq, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine-liberation-organization">Palestine Liberation Organization</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> supported the Eritreans to varying degrees. Talks mediated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jimmy-carter">Jimmy Carter</a> in 1989 produced a deal that collapsed. At a conference in July 1991 the new Ethiopian transitional government recognised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a>'s right to a referendum. In April 1993 the Eritrean people voted almost unanimously for independence, and on 28 May 1993 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> admitted Eritrea to membership.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Eritrean War of Independence” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
